# Econ Lab, part B: economic indicators and the business cycle; business in society.
# Run by build_data.py in the same namespace as econ_a.py, so p() and P already exist.

# ======================= Economic indicators and the business cycle =======================
p("EC:017", "Gross Domestic Product (GDP)",
 ["Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is the total market value of all final goods and services produced within a country's borders in a period, usually a quarter or a year. It is the broadest measure of the size and health of an economy. Using the expenditure approach, GDP = C + I + G + (X - M): consumer spending, business investment, government purchases and net exports (exports minus imports).",
  "Nominal GDP uses current prices; real GDP adjusts for inflation, so it shows whether the economy actually produced more. GDP per capita divides GDP by population to compare living standards. In the U.S., the Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA) publishes GDP every quarter as an annualized growth rate, first as an advance estimate and later revised. GDP has limits: it leaves out unpaid work like caring for family, the underground economy, and measures of well-being such as leisure or the environment."],
 ["GDP = market value of all final goods and services produced in a country.",
  "GDP = C + I + G + (X - M).",
  "Real GDP removes inflation; nominal doesn't.",
  "GDP per capita compares living standards.",
  "Only final goods count, to avoid double counting."],
 ["Rising real GDP usually means rising company earnings, more hiring and fewer loan defaults.",
  "Banks use GDP forecasts to plan lending and set aside reserves for losses.",
  "Investors compare GDP growth across countries when deciding where to invest."],
 ["Real GDP grew at a 3.4% annual rate in the third quarter of 2026, up from 2.2% in the second quarter (BEA advance estimate, Oct. 29, 2026).",
  "U.S. nominal GDP was about $29 trillion in 2024, the largest in the world (BEA; IMF World Economic Outlook).",
  "Consumer spending makes up about 68% of U.S. GDP, by far the biggest component (BEA)."],
 "Don't count intermediate goods. The flour a bakery buys is already inside the price of the bread, so counting both would double count. And don't compare nominal GDP across years without adjusting for inflation.",
 "\"Real GDP grew at a 3.4% annual rate last quarter, so the economy is still expanding. That supports our client's plan to expand, but we'd still keep a cash cushion in case growth slows.\"",
 [("What does GDP growth tell our client about the economy?",
   "It tells us whether the economy is producing more, which drives jobs, wages and company profits. Real GDP grew at a 3.4% annual rate in the third quarter of 2026, which means the economy is expanding, not in recession. For our client, an expanding economy is a reasonable time to invest steadily and keep their job secure. But GDP looks backward, so we'd still build a three-to-six-month emergency fund in case growth slows."),
  ("Why does a bank care about GDP?",
   "Because GDP growth drives loan demand and loan quality. When the economy grows, businesses borrow to expand and borrowers repay on time; when GDP shrinks, defaults rise. Since consumer spending is about 68% of GDP, a slowdown hits a bank's card and auto loans first. So a bank watches GDP to decide how fast to grow lending and how much to set aside for losses, which protects depositors and shareholders.")],
 [("GDP","The total market value of all final goods and services produced within a country in a period.","GDP = C + I + G + (X - M)."),
  ("Real vs nominal GDP","Real GDP is adjusted for inflation; nominal GDP uses current prices.","Use real GDP to tell if output actually grew."),
  ("GDP per capita","GDP divided by population; a rough measure of living standards.","Lets you compare countries of different sizes.")])

p("EC:081", "Consumer spending as an economic indicator",
 ["Consumer spending is what households spend on goods and services, from groceries to rent to haircuts. In the U.S. it makes up about 68% of GDP, so it is the single biggest driver of the economy. When households spend more, businesses sell more, hire more and invest more; when households pull back, the slowdown spreads.",
  "Economists track it several ways. Personal consumption expenditures (PCE), from the BEA each month, measure total spending. Retail sales, from the Census Bureau, measure store and online sales and come out sooner. Surveys of consumer sentiment (University of Michigan) and consumer confidence (The Conference Board) measure how people feel, which can hint at future spending. The personal saving rate shows how much of after-tax income households save instead of spend."],
 ["Consumer spending is about 68% of U.S. GDP.",
  "Measured by PCE (BEA) and retail sales (Census).",
  "Sentiment and confidence surveys measure attitudes, which may predict spending.",
  "The saving rate shows the cushion households have.",
  "Real spending (inflation-adjusted) matters more than nominal."],
 ["Card issuers and banks see spending in real time through transaction data and watch for rising delinquencies.",
  "Retail and restaurant borrowers depend on consumer spending, so lenders watch it when underwriting.",
  "A low saving rate means households have less cushion if layoffs rise."],
 ["In September 2026, consumer spending (PCE) rose 0.5%, but after inflation real spending was nearly flat (less than 0.1%), and the personal saving rate was 3.8% (BEA, Oct. 29, 2026).",
  "The University of Michigan's consumer sentiment index rose to a preliminary 51.0 in November 2026 from 48.0 in October, after a record low of 44.8 in May 2026 (University of Michigan Surveys of Consumers, Nov. 2026).",
  "Consumer spending is about 68% of GDP (BEA)."],
 "Don't treat a rise in nominal spending as good news by itself. If prices rose as much as spending, people bought the same amount or less. Also, sentiment and spending don't always move together: people can feel gloomy and keep spending.",
 "\"Spending rose 0.5% in September, but after inflation it was nearly flat. That tells us families are paying more for the same things, which is why we'd keep this retailer's inventory lean.\"",
 [("Consumers say they feel gloomy but keep spending. Which should a business believe?",
   "Watch what people do more than what they say, but don't ignore the mood. Spending is the real data; sentiment is an early warning. In 2026, sentiment hit a record low of 44.8 in May while spending kept growing, but by September real spending was nearly flat and the saving rate was just 3.8%. That combination says households are stretched. So we'd tell this business to plan for slower sales: keep inventory lean and avoid big fixed commitments until spending picks up."),
  ("What would falling consumer spending mean for this retailer's loan?",
   "It would raise the risk that the retailer can't make payments, because about 68% of the economy is consumer spending and retailers feel a slowdown first. Lower sales with the same rent and payroll squeezes cash flow. So we'd stress-test the loan: could the business still pay if sales fell 15%? We'd also add a cash-reserve requirement and a line of credit so a slow season doesn't turn into a missed payment.")],
 [("Personal consumption expenditures (PCE)","The BEA's monthly measure of total household spending on goods and services.","About 68% of U.S. GDP."),
  ("Consumer sentiment","A survey measure of how consumers feel about their finances and the economy.","University of Michigan index: 51.0 prelim, Nov. 2026."),
  ("Personal saving rate","The share of after-tax income households save.","3.8% in September 2026 (BEA).")])

p("EC:082", "Unemployment rates",
 ["The unemployment rate is the percentage of the labor force that is jobless but actively looking for work. The labor force includes people who are working or looking; people who aren't looking, like retirees and full-time students, aren't counted. The Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) publishes the rate monthly in the Employment Situation report, along with how many jobs employers added.",
  "There are four main types of unemployment: frictional (between jobs or just entering the workforce), structural (skills don't match available jobs, often because of technology or industry change), cyclical (caused by a recession) and seasonal (jobs that disappear at certain times of year). Even a healthy economy has some unemployment. High unemployment means lost income, lower spending, lower tax revenue and more loan defaults. Very low unemployment can push wages up and make hiring hard for businesses."],
 ["Unemployment rate = unemployed ÷ labor force.",
  "To be unemployed you must be looking for work.",
  "Types: frictional, structural, cyclical, seasonal.",
  "Reported monthly by the BLS.",
  "Unemployment is a lagging indicator: it often rises after a recession starts."],
 ["Job loss is the biggest cause of missed loan payments, which is why advisors stress emergency funds.",
  "Banks raise loan-loss reserves when unemployment is expected to climb.",
  "Low unemployment gives workers bargaining power, raising wages and business costs."],
 ["The unemployment rate was 4.1% in October 2026, as employers added 165,000 jobs (BLS Employment Situation, Nov. 2026).",
  "Unemployment hit 14.8% in April 2020, the highest since the BLS began tracking in 1948, then fell quickly as the economy reopened (BLS).",
  "Structural change: as banks moved to mobile apps, demand for tellers fell while demand for software and cybersecurity staff grew (BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook)."],
 "Don't say everyone without a job is unemployed. Retirees, full-time students and people who stopped looking are outside the labor force. Also, 'full employment' doesn't mean 0% unemployment, because frictional unemployment always exists.",
 "\"Unemployment is 4.1%, so jobs are fairly plentiful, but layoffs can hit any industry. That's why step one for our client is three to six months of expenses in a high-yield savings account.\"",
 [("If our client lost their job tomorrow, what would protect them?",
   "An emergency fund first, then insurance and a plan. Job loss is the most common reason people fall behind on bills, so we'd build three to six months of expenses in a high-yield savings account. State unemployment benefits replace only part of pay for a limited time. Even with unemployment at 4.1% in October 2026, layoffs happen in every industry. So we'd automate savings now, keep credit card balances low and update the resume, so a layoff becomes a setback instead of a crisis."),
  ("Why does a low unemployment rate make it hard for this business to hire?",
   "Because when most people who want jobs already have one, employers compete for fewer available workers, which pushes wages up. With unemployment around 4%, this business is bidding against bigger firms. So we'd recommend competing on more than pay: flexible schedules, training and a clear path to promotion, plus a referral bonus. Raising productivity with better equipment also means the business needs fewer new hires to grow.")],
 [("Unemployment rate","The percentage of the labor force that is jobless and actively looking for work.","4.1% in October 2026 (BLS)."),
  ("Four types of unemployment","Frictional (between jobs), structural (skills mismatch), cyclical (recession), seasonal.","Tellers replaced by apps = structural."),
  ("Labor force","People 16 and older who are working or actively looking for work.","Retirees and full-time students aren't in it.")])

p("EC:083", "The economic impact of inflation on business",
 ["Inflation is a general rise in prices over time, which means each dollar buys less. In the U.S. it is measured mainly by the Consumer Price Index (CPI) from the BLS and the PCE price index from the BEA; the Federal Reserve targets 2% inflation, measured by PCE. Demand-pull inflation happens when spending grows faster than the economy can produce; cost-push inflation happens when costs like energy or wages rise.",
  "For businesses, inflation raises the cost of materials, energy, rent and wages. Owners must decide whether to raise prices and risk losing customers or absorb costs and accept lower margins. Inflation makes planning harder, because future costs are uncertain, and it usually leads to higher interest rates as the Fed responds, raising borrowing costs. Some effects help: borrowers with fixed-rate debt repay with dollars worth less, and inventory bought earlier can be sold at higher prices. Cash sitting idle loses buying power."],
 ["Inflation = general rise in prices; purchasing power falls.",
  "Measured by CPI (BLS) and PCE price index (BEA); Fed target 2%.",
  "Demand-pull vs cost-push.",
  "Business impact: higher costs, pricing decisions, wage pressure, higher rates.",
  "Fixed-rate borrowers benefit; savers in cash lose."],
 ["Lenders raise rates when inflation rises, so variable-rate business loans get more expensive.",
  "Advisors move idle cash into high-yield savings, Treasury bills or I bonds so it keeps up with inflation.",
  "Businesses review contracts: escalation clauses let prices rise with costs."],
 ["Consumer prices rose 3.7% in the 12 months through September 2026, above the Fed's 2% target; the PCE price index rose 3.6% (BLS; BEA, Oct. 2026).",
  "Cost-push example: gasoline topped $4.50 a gallon in spring 2026 amid shipping disruptions in the Strait of Hormuz, raising delivery and travel costs for businesses (BLS CPI releases; University of Michigan, 2026).",
  "CPI inflation peaked at 9.1% in June 2022, the highest in about 40 years (BLS)."],
 "Don't say inflation means 'prices are high.' It means prices are rising. If inflation falls from 4% to 3%, prices are still going up, just more slowly (that's disinflation; deflation is falling prices). And inflation doesn't hurt everyone equally: fixed-rate borrowers can gain.",
 "\"With inflation at 3.7%, the owner's costs are rising every month. Rather than a big price increase that scares customers off, we'd raise prices a little on the highest-demand items and lock in a fixed-rate loan now.\"",
 [("Should this business raise prices because of inflation?",
   "Probably yes, but carefully and selectively. With inflation at 3.7% in September 2026, the owner's costs are rising, and absorbing all of it shrinks margins. The key is demand: raise prices most on items customers value most and have few substitutes for, and less on price-sensitive items. Small, regular increases tend to cause less pushback than one big jump. We'd also cut waste and renegotiate with suppliers, so the business protects its margin without losing loyal customers."),
  ("How does inflation hurt a small business that has cash sitting in the bank?",
   "Cash earning little or nothing loses buying power every year. At 3.7% inflation, $50,000 in a 0.5% account buys about $1,600 less in a year. The business still needs some cash for operations and emergencies, but extra reserves should earn more. We'd move the reserve into a high-yield business savings account, a money market fund or short-term Treasury bills, keeping it safe and available while it keeps pace with rising costs.")],
 [("Inflation","A general rise in prices over time, which lowers the purchasing power of money.","3.7% CPI, 12 months through Sept. 2026 (BLS)."),
  ("Demand-pull vs cost-push inflation","Demand-pull: spending grows faster than output. Cost-push: input costs like energy or wages rise.","A gas price spike causes cost-push inflation."),
  ("Disinflation vs deflation","Disinflation: prices rise more slowly. Deflation: prices actually fall.","Inflation falling from 4% to 3% is disinflation.")])

p("EC:084", "The economic impact of interest-rate fluctuations",
 ["An interest rate is the price of borrowing money and the reward for saving it. The Federal Reserve sets a target range for the federal funds rate, the rate banks charge each other for overnight loans, and that ripples out to other rates. Many banks set their prime rate at the top of the Fed's range plus 3 percentage points, and credit cards, business lines of credit and home equity lines often float above prime. Longer-term rates, like 30-year mortgages, follow Treasury yields, which reflect expected inflation and growth.",
  "When rates rise, borrowing costs more, so households buy fewer homes and cars, businesses delay expansion, and spending slows, which helps cool inflation. Savers earn more. Prices of existing bonds fall, and stock valuations often drop. A higher rate can also strengthen the dollar. When rates fall, the opposite happens: borrowing and spending pick up, savers earn less, and existing bonds rise in value."],
 ["Interest rate = price of borrowing, reward for saving.",
  "The Fed sets the federal funds target range; other rates follow.",
  "Prime rate usually = top of the Fed range + 3 points.",
  "Higher rates: less borrowing and spending, more saving, lower bond prices, stronger dollar.",
  "Fixed vs variable rates decide who feels the change."],
 ["Variable-rate loans (credit cards, HELOCs, business lines of credit) reprice quickly when the Fed moves.",
  "Banks earn the spread between loan rates and deposit rates; rate changes squeeze or widen that margin.",
  "Advisors use rate cycles in advice: lock fixed rates when they're likely to rise, ladder CDs to balance yield and access."],
 ["The Fed raised its target range by 0.25 point to 3.75%-4.00% on Sept. 16, 2026 and held it on Oct. 28, 2026; its next meeting is Dec. 8-9, 2026 (Federal Reserve).",
  "That puts the prime rate at about 7.00% (the top of the Fed's range plus 3 points, the convention most banks use).",
  "The average 30-year fixed mortgage rate was about 7.11% for the week of Nov. 12, 2026 (Freddie Mac)."],
 "Don't say the Fed sets mortgage or credit card rates directly. It sets the federal funds target; other rates respond, and long-term rates also depend on markets. And remember bond prices move opposite to rates.",
 "\"The Fed raised rates to 3.75%-4.00% in September, so the owner's variable-rate line of credit now costs more. We'd move the long-term equipment balance into a fixed-rate term loan so future hikes can't raise the payment.\"",
 [("Should this business borrow now or wait for rates to fall?",
   "Decide based on the project's return, not a rate forecast, because nobody can time rates reliably. If the new equipment earns more than the loan costs, waiting means losing that profit. With the prime rate around 7%, we'd borrow only what the project needs, choose a fixed rate so the payment can't rise, and make sure there's no prepayment penalty. If rates fall later, the business can refinance. That captures the opportunity now while limiting rate risk."),
  ("How do rising rates help or hurt our client?",
   "It depends which side of the rate they're on. As a saver, our client earns more: high-yield savings and CDs pay more when the Fed raises rates. As a borrower, especially with variable-rate debt like credit cards, they pay more, since card rates move with prime. So we'd pay down the credit card balance first, since it's the most expensive debt, and lock some savings into a CD ladder to earn today's higher rates.")],
 [("Federal funds rate","The rate banks charge each other for overnight loans; the Fed sets a target range.","3.75%-4.00% after Sept. 16, 2026."),
  ("Prime rate","The base rate banks charge their best borrowers, usually the top of the Fed's range + 3 points.","About 7.00% in Nov. 2026."),
  ("Rates and bond prices","When interest rates rise, prices of existing bonds fall, and vice versa.","Long-term bonds fall the most.")])

p("EC:018", "Business cycles",
 ["The business cycle is the repeated rise and fall of economic activity over time. It has four phases. In an expansion, GDP grows, unemployment falls, and businesses hire and invest. At the peak, growth tops out, often with rising inflation. In a contraction, GDP falls, unemployment rises and spending slows; a deep, lasting contraction is a recession, and a severe one is a depression. At the trough, the economy bottoms out, then recovery begins and a new expansion starts.",
  "In the U.S., the National Bureau of Economic Research (NBER) officially dates recessions, looking at many indicators, not just two quarters of falling GDP. Cycles vary in length: the 2020 recession lasted only two months, while the Great Recession lasted 18 months. Businesses respond differently in each phase, and industries differ: cyclical industries (cars, travel, home building, luxury goods) swing with the cycle, while defensive industries (groceries, utilities, health care) stay steadier."],
 ["Phases: expansion, peak, contraction (recession), trough, then recovery.",
  "NBER officially dates U.S. recessions.",
  "Cyclical industries swing hard; defensive industries stay steady.",
  "Indicators: leading (stock prices, building permits), coincident (GDP, payrolls), lagging (unemployment).",
  "Businesses expand in good times and protect cash in downturns."],
 ["Lenders tighten credit near peaks and in contractions, so businesses should line up credit before they need it.",
  "Advisors use the cycle to explain why diversified portfolios and emergency funds matter: downturns are normal, not rare.",
  "Banks build loan-loss reserves ahead of expected downturns."],
 ["The Great Recession lasted 18 months (December 2007 to June 2009); the 2020 recession lasted two months (February to April 2020), the shortest on record (NBER).",
  "Today: real GDP grew at a 3.4% annual rate in Q3 2026 and unemployment was 4.1% in October, consistent with an expansion (BEA; BLS).",
  "Defensive vs cyclical: grocery chains held up through 2008-09 while home builders and automakers cut deeply."],
 "Don't define a recession only as 'two quarters of negative GDP.' That's a rule of thumb; the NBER uses a broader set of data. And don't promise when the next recession will come. Nobody can time the cycle reliably.",
 "\"The economy is in an expansion now, with 3.4% GDP growth, but every expansion ends eventually. So we'd use the good times to build cash reserves and open a line of credit before this business needs it.\"",
 [("How should this business prepare for the next recession?",
   "Prepare in the expansion, because banks lend most easily when times are good. First, build cash reserves of three to six months of fixed costs. Second, open a line of credit now, even if unused. Third, keep some costs flexible, like part-time staff or month-to-month contracts. In 2008-09, businesses with cash and credit lines survived while others closed. With GDP growing 3.4% in late 2026, now is the time to set this up, so a downturn becomes a challenge instead of a crisis."),
  ("Which phase of the business cycle are we in right now, and why does it matter?",
   "The data point to an expansion: real GDP grew at a 3.4% annual rate in the third quarter of 2026 and unemployment was 4.1%. But inflation is 3.7% and the Fed raised rates in September, which are signs of a late-stage expansion. It matters because the right moves differ by phase. For this client, it means borrowing carefully at fixed rates, avoiding stretched expansion plans and building reserves now, while credit is still available.")],
 [("Business cycle","The repeated rise and fall of economic activity: expansion, peak, contraction, trough.","NBER dates U.S. recessions."),
  ("Recession","A significant, widespread decline in economic activity lasting more than a few months.","2007-09 lasted 18 months; 2020 lasted 2."),
  ("Cyclical vs defensive industries","Cyclical industries swing with the economy; defensive industries stay steady.","Cars and travel vs groceries and utilities.")])

# ======================= Business in society =======================
p("EC:070", "The role of business in society",
 ["Businesses exist to produce the goods and services people want, but their role in society is bigger than that. They create jobs and income, pay taxes that fund schools and roads, develop new products and technologies, create returns for investors and savers, and support communities through local spending and giving. In return, society expects businesses to obey laws, treat workers and customers fairly and limit harm to the environment.",
  "Social responsibility is the idea that businesses have obligations to all stakeholders, not only owners. Stakeholders include customers, employees, suppliers, the community, government and shareholders. In 2019 the Business Roundtable, a group of major company CEOs, changed its statement on the purpose of a corporation to commit to all stakeholders rather than shareholders alone."],
 ["Business provides goods, services, jobs, income and taxes.",
  "Businesses drive innovation and economic growth.",
  "Stakeholders: customers, employees, suppliers, community, government, owners.",
  "Social responsibility: obligations beyond profit.",
  "Profit and responsibility can support each other."],
 ["Banks channel savings into loans that build homes, businesses and communities.",
  "The Community Reinvestment Act of 1977 requires banks to help meet the credit needs of the communities they serve, including low- and moderate-income neighborhoods.",
  "Financial education programs build trust and future customers."],
 ["The Community Reinvestment Act (1977) requires federally insured banks to serve the credit needs of their whole communities, and regulators rate how well they do (FDIC; OCC; Federal Reserve).",
  "In 2019, the Business Roundtable's new 'Statement on the Purpose of a Corporation' committed member CEOs to customers, employees, suppliers, communities and shareholders (Business Roundtable).",
  "Small businesses employ about 46% of U.S. workers (SBA Office of Advocacy, 2025)."],
 "Don't frame it as profit versus society. Profitable businesses create jobs and taxes, and responsible practices often build loyalty that raises profit. The strong answer shows how both work together.",
 "\"A bank's role goes beyond earning interest. When we lend to local businesses and teach financial literacy, we create jobs and future customers, and the Community Reinvestment Act expects us to.\"",
 [("Why should a bank care about the community and not just profit?",
   "Because a bank's profit depends on the community's health. A bank grows when local businesses and households do well, since they're its borrowers and depositors. It's also the law: the Community Reinvestment Act requires banks to meet local credit needs, and regulators rate them on it. For example, a small-business loan program or free financial literacy workshops build loyalty and new customers. So community investment is a smart long-term business strategy, not charity."),
  ("How does this business benefit the town beyond its owners?",
   "It creates jobs, pays local taxes, buys from local suppliers and gives residents a service close to home. Every dollar it pays in wages gets spent again in town, at the grocery store and the gas station. Local businesses also sponsor teams and events, which builds community ties. That's why helping this owner grow, with a well-sized loan and a solid budget, benefits the whole local economy, and it's a strong reason for the bank to support it.")],
 [("Stakeholders","Everyone affected by a business: customers, employees, suppliers, community, government and owners.","Business Roundtable 2019 statement."),
  ("Social responsibility","A business's obligation to act in ways that benefit society, not only its owners.","Fair treatment, community investment, limiting harm."),
  ("Community Reinvestment Act (1977)","Requires banks to help meet the credit needs of the communities they serve.","Includes low- and moderate-income areas.")])

p("EC:071", "Types of business activities",
 ["Every business, from a lemonade stand to a global bank, performs four basic activities. Production creates or obtains the goods and services the business sells. Marketing figures out what customers want and gets products to them: pricing, promotion, selling and distribution. Finance obtains and manages money: budgeting, raising funds, paying bills, recordkeeping and planning for growth. Management plans, organizes, staffs and controls the work so the other activities run well. (Accounting and human resources are often listed too.)",
  "Businesses are also grouped by what they do: extractors (farming, mining, fishing), producers or manufacturers, service businesses (banks, salons, consultants) and intermediaries such as wholesalers and retailers who move products from producers to customers. A business's success depends on all four activities working together."],
 ["Four core activities: production, marketing, finance, management.",
  "Finance = obtaining and managing money.",
  "Business types: extractive, manufacturing, service, intermediaries (wholesale, retail).",
  "Activities depend on each other.",
  "Weakness in one activity (like finance) can sink a strong product."],
 ["Financial services firms are service businesses, and their production is the service itself: processing payments, managing accounts and giving advice.",
  "The finance function inside any company decides how to fund growth: savings, loans or investors.",
  "Lenders evaluate all four activities in a business plan, not just the numbers."],
 ["Cash-flow failures: many profitable small businesses fail because the finance activity breaks down, with bills due before customers pay (SBA guidance on cash flow).",
  "Banks as service businesses: their 'product' is a set of services such as deposits, loans, payments and advice.",
  "Intermediaries: wholesalers and retailers add place and time utility by moving goods from producers to customers."],
 "Don't treat finance as just 'accounting.' Accounting records what happened; finance decides how to obtain and use money going forward. And production isn't only for factories: a service business 'produces' its service.",
 "\"This owner is great at production and marketing, but the finance activity is missing: no budget, no cash forecast. Our plan adds a 13-week cash flow forecast so strong sales turn into money in the bank.\"",
 [("Which business activity should this owner focus on first?",
   "Finance, because it decides whether the business survives long enough for the others to succeed. The product is good and customers are coming, but without a budget and cash flow plan, bills can come due before customers pay. That's how profitable businesses run out of cash. So we'd start with a monthly budget, a 13-week cash forecast and a separate business account. Then we'd use the extra cash to fund marketing that grows sales."),
  ("How does the finance function support marketing and production?",
   "Finance provides the money and the numbers the other activities need. It funds production with equipment loans or working capital, and it sets marketing budgets based on what each campaign returns. For example, before launching a $5,000 ad campaign, finance checks how many new sales it needs to pay for itself. When the three work together, the business spends on what makes money and avoids running short of cash.")],
 [("Four business activities","Production, marketing, finance and management.","All four must work together."),
  ("Finance (business activity)","Obtaining and managing money: budgeting, funding, paying bills, recordkeeping and planning.","Different from accounting, which records results."),
  ("Intermediary","A business that moves products from producers to customers, such as a wholesaler or retailer.","Adds place and time utility.")])

p("EC:138", "Types of business models",
 ["A business model explains how a company creates value for customers, delivers it and captures value as revenue and profit. Common models include: product sales (one-time purchases), subscription (recurring fees, like streaming services), freemium (free basic product, paid upgrades), advertising (free to users, paid by advertisers), marketplace or platform (connecting buyers and sellers for a fee), franchise (selling the right to use a brand and system), licensing, membership (a fee for access, like a warehouse club), razor-and-blade (cheap base product, profitable refills) and pay-per-use.",
  "Financial services have their own models. Traditional banks earn the spread between loan and deposit rates (net interest income) plus fees. Advisors may charge a percentage of assets under management (often around 1% a year), a flat fee, an hourly rate or commissions. Card networks and many fintechs earn interchange fees, a small percentage of each card purchase paid by merchants."],
 ["Business model = how a company creates, delivers and captures value.",
  "Common models: product, subscription, freemium, advertising, marketplace, franchise, membership, licensing.",
  "Banks: interest spread + fees.",
  "Advisors: AUM percentage, flat fee, hourly or commission.",
  "Recurring revenue is usually more predictable and valued more by lenders and investors."],
 ["Lenders like recurring-revenue models (subscriptions, memberships) because cash flow is steadier.",
  "The way an advisor is paid creates incentives and possible conflicts, which must be disclosed.",
  "Fintechs often use different models than banks, such as earning mostly from interchange fees instead of interest."],
 ["Membership model: Costco earned about $4.8 billion in membership fees in fiscal 2024 (Costco 2024 Annual Report).",
  "Interchange model: Chime's 2025 IPO filing showed most of its revenue came from interchange fees on members' debit card purchases, not loan interest (Chime Financial Form S-1, 2025).",
  "Fee models compared: on a $500,000 account, a 1% AUM fee costs $5,000 a year, while a flat-fee planner might charge a fixed annual amount regardless of account size."],
 "Don't confuse a business model with a business type or industry. Two companies in the same industry can use different models: one bank earns mainly from interest, a fintech mostly from interchange fees. Name how the money comes in.",
 "\"Our client's gym has a product-sales model: pay per visit. Switching to a monthly membership creates recurring revenue, which steadies cash flow and makes the business easier to finance.\"",
 [("How does this company actually make money?",
   "We'd start with the business model: who pays, for what and how often. For example, a bank mainly earns the spread between what it charges borrowers and pays depositors, plus fees, while a fintech like Chime earns mostly interchange fees when members use their debit cards. Knowing the model shows what drives profit and what could threaten it. For our client, that tells us which numbers to watch, like how many active card users the business keeps."),
  ("Should our firm charge a flat fee or a percentage of assets?",
   "It depends on the clients we serve, but a flat fee is often clearer for younger or smaller accounts. A percentage of assets, often around 1%, grows with the account, which aligns us with growth but can get expensive: $5,000 a year on $500,000. A flat fee is predictable and fair for clients who need planning more than investment management. Many firms offer both. Whatever we choose, we'd disclose it in writing so clients know exactly what they pay and why.")],
 [("Business model","How a company creates, delivers and captures value as revenue and profit.","Subscription, freemium, marketplace, membership..."),
  ("Recurring revenue","Revenue from repeat payments, such as subscriptions or memberships.","Steadier cash flow, easier to finance."),
  ("Interchange fee","A small percentage of each card purchase paid by the merchant to the card issuer.","Main revenue source for many fintechs.")])

p("EC:103", "Organizational design of businesses",
 ["Organizational design is how a business arranges its people, jobs and authority to reach its goals. It is shown on an organizational chart. Key ideas include the chain of command (who reports to whom), span of control (how many people one manager supervises), and centralization (decisions made at the top) versus decentralization (decisions pushed down to managers closer to customers).",
  "Common structures include line (direct authority from top to bottom), line-and-staff (line managers plus specialist staff such as legal or HR who advise), functional (grouped by function: finance, marketing, operations), divisional (grouped by product, geography or customer type), matrix (employees report to both a functional and a project manager) and team-based. Tall organizations have many layers and narrow spans of control; flat organizations have few layers and wide spans, which speeds decisions."],
 ["Org chart shows the chain of command.",
  "Span of control: number of people one manager supervises.",
  "Tall (many layers) vs flat (few layers).",
  "Structures: line, line-and-staff, functional, divisional, matrix, team.",
  "Centralized vs decentralized decision making."],
 ["Large banks are divisional, organized by customer: consumer banking, commercial banking, wealth management and investment banking.",
  "Compliance and risk are often 'staff' roles that advise and check the line businesses.",
  "Fintechs tend to be flat, which speeds product changes; big banks are taller, which adds control."],
 ["Divisional design: in 2024, JPMorgan Chase combined its commercial bank and its corporate and investment bank into one Commercial & Investment Bank, alongside Consumer & Community Banking and Asset & Wealth Management (JPMorgan Chase, 2024).",
  "A growing small business often moves from the owner supervising everyone (line) to department leads for sales, operations and finance (functional).",
  "Matrix example: a bank's product launch team pulls staff from marketing, IT and compliance, who also report to their own department heads."],
 "Don't assume flatter is always better. Flat structures speed decisions but can overload managers and blur accountability. In a regulated business like banking, some layers exist for control and compliance.",
 "\"With 25 employees all reporting to the owner, the span of control is too wide. We'd recommend a functional structure with three leads, for sales, operations and finance, so the owner can focus on growth.\"",
 [("How should this growing business organize its employees?",
   "Move from everyone reporting to the owner to a simple functional structure. Right now the owner's span of control is too wide, so decisions bottleneck. We'd create three leads, for sales, operations and finance, each managing a small team, and draw a clear org chart. As the business adds locations, it could become divisional by location. That frees the owner to focus on strategy and banking relationships while each lead owns results in their area."),
  ("Who should make decisions: the owner or the managers?",
   "Big, risky decisions should stay with the owner, and everyday decisions should move to the managers closest to customers. That's a mix of centralized and decentralized decision making. For example, managers could approve refunds up to $200 or adjust schedules, while the owner approves loans and major purchases. Decentralizing daily calls speeds service and develops managers, and keeping major decisions central protects the business. We'd write the limits down so everyone knows who decides what.")],
 [("Span of control","The number of employees a manager directly supervises.","Wide span = flat organization."),
  ("Line-and-staff structure","Line managers have direct authority; staff specialists (legal, HR) advise them.","Compliance is often a staff role in banks."),
  ("Divisional structure","Organizing by product, geography or customer type.","Consumer banking vs wealth management divisions.")])

p("EC:105", "Factors that affect the business environment",
 ["The business environment is everything inside and outside a company that affects how it operates. The internal environment includes the company's employees, culture, finances, resources and management. The external environment includes forces the company can't control but must respond to. A common tool to scan them is PESTLE: Political (government stability, trade policy, taxes), Economic (growth, inflation, interest rates, unemployment), Social (demographics, values, lifestyles), Technological (new tools, AI, automation), Legal (laws and regulations) and Environmental (climate, weather, sustainability).",
  "Businesses also watch competitors, customers and suppliers. A SWOT analysis combines both views: strengths and weaknesses are internal, opportunities and threats are external. Businesses that scan the environment regularly can adapt before changes hurt them."],
 ["Internal vs external environment.",
  "PESTLE: political, economic, social, technological, legal, environmental.",
  "Also: competitors, customers, suppliers.",
  "SWOT links internal strengths/weaknesses to external opportunities/threats.",
  "Environmental scanning helps businesses adapt early."],
 ["Banks scan the economic environment (rates, unemployment) to set lending standards.",
  "Social trends shape financial products: an aging population drives demand for retirement income planning.",
  "Technology and legal changes, such as new data and AI rules, change how firms handle customer information."],
 ["Social factor: more than 11,200 Americans a day turn 65 from 2024 through 2027, the 'Peak 65' years, boosting demand for retirement income advice (Alliance for Lifetime Income).",
  "Economic factor: the Fed raised rates to 3.75%-4.00% in September 2026 while inflation ran at 3.7% (Federal Reserve; BLS).",
  "Political factor: the U.S. declined to renew USMCA at its July 1, 2026 joint review, starting annual reviews and adding uncertainty for North American supply chains (USTR, July 2026)."],
 "Don't list PESTLE factors without connecting them to the client. Judges want to hear which one or two factors matter most for this business and what to do about them.",
 "\"Of all the outside forces, two matter most for this business: rising interest rates, which raise its loan costs, and an aging customer base. Our plan addresses both.\"",
 [("What outside factors could hurt this business over the next few years?",
   "Using PESTLE, the biggest threats are economic and political. Economically, rates rose to 3.75%-4.00% in September 2026, which raises the cost of its variable-rate loan, and inflation at 3.7% raises costs. Politically, trade rules are uncertain: tariffs changed after the Supreme Court's February 2026 ruling, and USMCA now faces annual reviews. So we'd fix the loan rate, find a second supplier and review prices quarterly, so the business can adapt as things change."),
  ("How would you scan the environment before giving this advice?",
   "We'd use a PESTLE scan, then a SWOT. PESTLE checks political, economic, social, technological, legal and environmental forces. We'd look at current data, like the BLS inflation report and the Fed's latest decision, plus local trends and competitors. Then SWOT matches the client's strengths to the opportunities we found. That makes our recommendation fit today's conditions, not last year's, and gives our client a list of signals to watch.")],
 [("PESTLE","A scan of Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Legal and Environmental factors.","Tool for the external environment."),
  ("Internal vs external environment","Internal: factors inside the company (staff, finances). External: outside forces it can't control.","Interest rates are external."),
  ("SWOT analysis","Strengths and weaknesses (internal) plus opportunities and threats (external).","Connects the company to its environment.")])

p("EC:106", "The nature of business ethics",
 ["Business ethics are the moral principles that guide how a business and its people behave: honesty, fairness, respect, responsibility and keeping promises. Ethics go further than the law. Something can be legal but still unethical, like a misleading sales tactic that technically discloses the fee in fine print. Ethical issues in business include conflicts of interest, honesty in advertising, fair treatment of employees and customers, privacy, accurate financial reporting and bribery.",
  "Companies set expectations through a code of ethics and training, and many have hotlines so employees can report problems; laws protect certain whistleblowers. Simple tests help with decisions: Is it legal? Is it fair to everyone affected? Would I be comfortable if it appeared in the news or if my family knew? In finance, ethics are central, because the whole business depends on trust in people handling other people's money."],
 ["Ethics = moral principles guiding business behavior.",
  "Legal is not always ethical.",
  "Issues: conflicts of interest, honesty, privacy, fair treatment, accurate reporting.",
  "Tools: code of ethics, training, hotlines, whistleblower protections.",
  "Decision tests: legal, fair, the newspaper test."],
 ["Financial advisors must manage conflicts of interest and disclose how they're paid.",
  "Sales goals can push unethical behavior if they reward volume over customer benefit.",
  "Ethical failures in finance lead to huge fines, lost customers and damaged trust in the whole industry."],
 ["Wells Fargo employees, under pressure to meet sales goals, opened millions of accounts customers hadn't authorized; the bank agreed to pay $3 billion in 2020 to settle criminal and civil investigations (U.S. Department of Justice, 2020).",
  "The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002, passed after the Enron and WorldCom scandals, requires CEOs and CFOs to certify financial statements and protects whistleblowers (SEC).",
  "Many firms use the 'newspaper test': would you be comfortable seeing the decision on the front page?"],
 "Don't answer an ethics question with 'it's legal, so it's fine.' Judges want to hear that ethics go beyond the law and how you'd protect the customer's interests and the firm's reputation.",
 "\"Even if it's technically legal, it isn't right for this client. Ethics come before short-term sales: Wells Fargo paid $3 billion after pressure to hit sales goals led to unauthorized accounts.\"",
 [("What would you do if a customer asked you to bend a rule for them?",
   "I'd say no politely, explain why, and offer a legitimate alternative. Rules in finance protect customers, the bank and the financial system, so bending them for one person isn't fair to everyone else and could be illegal. For example, if a customer asked me to skip identity verification to open an account faster, I'd explain it protects them from fraud and help them finish it quickly. Being firm and helpful at the same time keeps the customer's trust and the firm's integrity."),
  ("Why is ethics a business issue and not just a personal one?",
   "Because unethical behavior destroys trust, and trust is what customers buy from a financial firm. When Wells Fargo's sales pressure led employees to open unauthorized accounts, the bank paid $3 billion in 2020, lost customers and faced years of restrictions. Ethics also lower risk: fewer lawsuits, fines and turnover. So a firm builds ethics into its system, with fair sales goals, training and a hotline, not just hoping people do the right thing.")],
 [("Business ethics","Moral principles of honesty, fairness and responsibility guiding business behavior.","Legal is not always ethical."),
  ("Conflict of interest","A situation where personal gain could influence a decision that should serve someone else.","An advisor earning more for selling one product."),
  ("Whistleblower","An employee who reports illegal or unethical conduct; certain laws protect them.","Sarbanes-Oxley (2002) protections.")])

p("EC:107", "How organizations adapt to today's markets",
 ["Markets change constantly with new technology, new competitors, new customer expectations and new rules. Organizations that survive adapt. They listen to customers through feedback and data, test new ideas quickly and in small ways, invest in technology and digital channels, train employees in new skills, form partnerships, and sometimes change their business model entirely (a pivot). Agile organizations make decisions quickly and adjust as they learn.",
  "Adapting also means stopping things: closing underused locations, dropping products customers no longer want or leaving markets. Companies that fail to adapt lose customers to rivals who do. Today's big shifts include mobile and digital services, artificial intelligence, remote and hybrid work, sustainability expectations and faster payments."],
 ["Adapt through customer feedback, data, technology, training and partnerships.",
  "Pivot = changing the business model or main product.",
  "Agile = quick decisions, small tests, continuous improvement.",
  "Adapting includes stopping what no longer works.",
  "Current shifts: mobile, AI, hybrid work, sustainability, real-time payments."],
 ["Banks shifted from branches to apps; the app is now customers' top banking channel.",
  "Banks partner with fintechs or build their own digital tools to keep younger customers.",
  "Advisors adapted with virtual meetings and planning portals, reaching clients anywhere."],
 ["Mobile shift: in an October 2026 survey, 44% of bank customers named a mobile app as their top way to manage their account, more than any other method, while about 10% preferred visiting a branch (American Bankers Association/Morning Consult, Nov. 2026).",
  "Pivot: Netflix moved from mailing DVDs to streaming, and shipped its final DVD in 2023 (Netflix).",
  "Investment in change: JPMorgan Chase spends close to $18 billion a year on technology (JPMorgan Chase investor materials, 2025)."],
 "Don't treat adapting as 'just add an app.' Judges want a plan: what customers need, what to change first, how to test it and how to measure results.",
 "\"More customers manage their bank accounts by app than by any other method. Our plan adapts the bank by redesigning the app first, then turning two underused branches into advice centers for bigger decisions like mortgages.\"",
 [("How should a traditional bank adapt to customers who never visit a branch?",
   "Meet them on their phones, and turn branches into places for big decisions. In the ABA's October 2026 survey, 44% of customers named a mobile app as their top way to bank, more than any other method, and only about 10% preferred a branch. So we'd invest first in the app: fast account opening, instant transfers, alerts and in-app chat with a real person. Then we'd convert low-traffic branches into advice centers for mortgages, small-business loans and planning. That cuts costs and keeps the human relationship where it matters most."),
  ("What should this business change first to keep up with its market?",
   "Start with what customers are asking for, then test small. We'd look at customer feedback and sales data to find the biggest gap, like no online ordering, and pilot a fix for one month before rolling it out. Netflix adapted by moving to streaming before DVDs died. For our client, a low-cost first step like online booking or mobile payments could keep customers from switching, and we'd measure results before investing more.")],
 [("Pivot","A major change in a company's business model or main product.","Netflix: DVDs by mail to streaming."),
  ("Agile organization","A business that makes quick decisions, tests in small steps and adjusts as it learns.","Pilot first, then roll out."),
  ("Digital transformation","Using technology to change how a business serves customers and operates.","Banks shifting from branches to apps.")])
